// Reports/tree-rewrite-design.md's replacement for the old TreeState-based
// continuous-position space colonization -- emergent, per-cell growth
// instead: a GrowingTip picks among its open 8-neighbours, weighted by
// local field reads (light, wind) plus a canopy-density self-avoidance
// term (deposit -> diffuse -> decay -> follow, not a private attractor
// point cloud), gated on locally diffusing resource rather than an
// unbounded energy pool. See that report for the full reasoning; this
// file is data, not a second place to re-derive it.
//
// GrowingTip carries both Grow and Photosynthesize -- this pass's Grow
// only ever creates more of its own parent's cell type (no separate Leaf
// spawned), so an actively growing shoot is also its own light-catching
// surface, the same way a real young shoot photosynthesizes before it
// lignifies into bark-covered wood. CellType::Leaf stays defined in
// organism.rs for a future species that wants a real distinct leaf stage;
// this file doesn't need it to be a tree.
//
// Every numeric value below is a first pass, deliberately -- resource now
// lives on a bounded 0..RESOURCE_SCALE (4.0) scale, not the old TreeState's
// unbounded energy pool, so the old constants (GROWTH_COST: 4.0,
// AMBIENT_GROWTH_ENERGY: 1.2, ROOT_WATER_ENERGY: 5.0, ...) don't transfer
// directly -- re-tuned for the new scale, calibrated against what actually
// grows in Reports/tree-rewrite-design.md's own live-verification step
// (step 6 of its retrofit order), not assumed correct on paper.
//
// GrowingTip's `Grow.cost`/`Photosynthesize.rate` (0.4/0.35 originally)
// were re-tuned after `examples/debug_tree_variants.rs` compared 6
// candidate pairs planted side by side in one scene, run for 20,000 ticks
// (~5.5 of field.rs's own day/night cycles, not a single lighting
// snapshot): 0.4/0.35 plateaued at 10 wood cells and never branched: even
// doubling income alone (0.4/0.55) still plateaued at 10, but lowering
// cost alone (0.25/0.35) reached 16 -- cost was the dominant lever, not
// average income, because a fresh cell's very first Grow check always
// reads resource=0 and Grow runs *before* Photosynthesize each tick, so a
// lower cost mainly buys margin against an unlucky low-light tick rather
// than raising the average. 0.2/0.5 won clearly (30 cells, real branching
// -- two Y-forks, not just a longer twig) and is what's below. Still a
// first pass in the sense that matters most: tuned entirely in one fixed
// test room (`examples/debug_tree_v2.rs`'s stone floor near the world's
// own sky boundary) with no other trees casting shade -- worth
// re-validating once real worldgen exists and light exposure varies by
// terrain rather than "how close to y=20 is the floor."
(
    name: "tree",
    cell_types: [
        (Seed, [
            Germinate(
                light_threshold: 0.1,
                moisture_threshold: 0.0,
                instant: false,
            ),
        ]),
        (GrowingTip, [
            Grow(
                cost: 0.2,
                branch_chance: 0.1,
                continuation_weight: 0.7,
                light_weight: 0.4,
                wind_weight: 0.2,
                upward_weight: 0.1,
                crowding_weight: 0.5,
                max_active_tips: 14,
                // Every 3rd growth step along a shoot leaves a Leaf behind.
                // Untuned -- picked as the smallest interval that still
                // reads as "leaves spaced along a stem" rather than "every
                // cell is a leaf", and deliberately not tuned here: the one
                // economy pass this phase gets happens after polarity lands
                // (Reports/plant-substrate-v2-design.md §10 step 3), and
                // tuning it twice is exactly what that sequencing exists to
                // avoid.
                plastochron: 3,
                // A shoot only grows into open air. Reports/tree-rewrite-
                // design.md §5 scopes growing-into-material to roots.
                penetration_force: 0.0,
                // Turgor-limited height. See Behavior::Grow's own docs and
                // Reports/tree-extension-biology.md §2c: a shoot apex is
                // limited by water potential falling with height, not by
                // age or by running out of carbon.
                //
                // These three give a DERIVED ceiling rather than a cap:
                //   h_max = (source - yield) / per_cell = 0.9 / 0.0075 = 120 rows
                // Chosen so a mature tree sits well inside the harness sky
                // (200 rows) instead of pinning against the world's top
                // edge, which every scene has done so far and which voids
                // any shape number taken from it.
                turgor_source: 1.0,
                turgor_yield: 0.1,
                turgor_per_cell: 0.0075,
            ),
            Photosynthesize(rate: 0.5),
        ]),
        (RootTip, [
            Absorb(rate: 1.5),
            Transpire(rate: 1.0),
            Grow(
                cost: 0.25,
                branch_chance: 0.04,
                continuation_weight: 0.7,
                light_weight: 0.0,
                wind_weight: 0.0,
                upward_weight: 0.6,
                crowding_weight: 0.0,
                max_active_tips: 10,
                // Roots grow by the same rule and must never leaf
                // underground -- 0 disables the mechanism entirely, which is
                // a real value here rather than "unset". See the field's own
                // doc in organism.rs.
                plastochron: 0,
                // Real root axial growth pressure is on the order of
                // 0.2-1.5 MPa; 1.2 puts this tree through loose soil (0.8)
                // and sand (1.4 -- refused), and never through gravel
                // (3.5) or any Solid. See Material::penetration_resistance.
                penetration_force: 1.2,
            ),
        ]),
        (MatureBody, [
            // **Re-derived when the gate's denominator was corrected**, and
            // the old 10.0 is not comparable. `thicken` used to divide the
            // foliage above by the *whole row's* organism cells; it now
            // divides by this stem's own contiguous run of woody cells,
            // which is what the pipe model names. A branched tree has
            // several runs across 53% of its occupied rows, so the old
            // denominator over-read by roughly the run count and 10.0 was
            // absorbing that factor. Left unchanged, the correction alone
            // took the stand from 4,624 cells to 12,985 -- ropy columns.
            //
            // Swept, 8 trees / 30,000 frames, mean cells / stand leaves /
            // median stem thickness above the base:
            //
            //     row-total 10   578   393    9    (the old baseline)
            //     per-stem  10  1623   299   19    blob
            //     per-stem  20   780   353    9
            //     per-stem  30   657   455    9
            //     per-stem  45   635   521    8    <-- here
            //     per-stem  60   514   555    7
            //     per-stem  90   450   626    7
            //
            // Monotone -- there is no interior optimum, so this is a shape
            // knob picked by eye against the baseline it replaces. 45 is
            // the value that reproduces the old stand's mass and trunk
            // width (635 against 578; 48% of rows wider than one cell
            // against 45%) while carrying a third more foliage, and it is
            // the first setting to produce a tree with a bare bole, a
            // clear fork and spreading limbs rather than a tapered spike.
            //
            // Girth is now bounded by crown size, which is the coupling the
            // pipe model exists for: trunks look thin because the crown is
            // sparse, and they will thicken on their own when it fills.
            // Do not chase trunk width by lowering this.
            SecondaryThicken(pipe_ratio: 45.0),
            StructuralAnchor,
            // Mature root tissue transpires. Harmless on canopy MatureBody
            // cells, which never have a soil neighbour to draw from -- see
            // Behavior::Transpire.
            Transpire(rate: 1.0),
        ]),
        (Leaf, [
            Photosynthesize(rate: 0.35),
        ]),
    ],
)
